// A wildcard-only relation (`viewer: user:*`, no plain `user` entry)
// combined with genuine recursion (`parent->view`) and an exclusion
// (`can_view = viewer - blocked`) — deliberately forces §7's bounded
// search: the non-recursive SMT tier declines outright on `isRecursive`,
// and the CHC tier declines the moment it meets the `exclusionBase`/
// `exclusionSubtract` pair (see `../../src/smt/chc-encode.ts`'s own
// header comment), so nothing decides `view` before the bounded fallback
// does.
//
// This is the exact shape the wildcard-soundness investigation
// (docs/DECISIONS.md, the entry following D-171) used to demonstrate a
// real, then-latent gap: before that entry's fix,
// `generateCandidateTuples` never constructed the literal `'*'` sentinel
// for a wildcard-declared subject type, so the ONLY way this schema's one
// real violation (nobody named `blocked`, and `viewer` grants to
// everyone) could ever be witnessed was a concrete stand-in grant
// (`folder:o#viewer@user:s`) that a since-fixed, independent
// `src/store/tuples.ts` bug happened to accept. With both fixes in place,
// `viewer`'s only candidate is the real wildcard tuple
// (`folder:o#viewer@user:*`) — see `test/bounded.test.ts`'s own dedicated
// describe block for the regression this guards.
namespace user {
  relation self: user
}

namespace folder {
  relation parent: folder
  relation viewer: user:*
  relation blocked: user

  permission can_view = viewer - blocked
  permission view = can_view | parent->view
}
